('2d3f06af-4797-5f76-9baa-b977f73ee165'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The bacterial nucleoid contains:', '["Nuclear membrane","Circular DNA","Histone proteins","Multiple chromosomes","Linear chromosomes"]'::jsonb, 'Circular DNA', 'Bacteria have a single circular chromosome located in the nucleoid region.'),
('23e09c60-5e8a-5f59-9b0f-49aa257d6fd5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Plasmids are:', '["Linear DNA fragments","Circular extrachromosomal DNA","RNA molecules","Protein structures","Lipid granules"]'::jsonb, 'Circular extrachromosomal DNA', 'Plasmids are small circular extrachromosomal DNA molecules that replicate independently and often carry antibiotic resistance genes.'),
('8a27d483-8dc3-5b66-8e92-dd11a44d3529'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which bacterial structure is responsible for conjugation?', '["Pili","Plasmid","Flagella","Capsule","Spores"]'::jsonb, 'Pili', 'Sex pili (conjugation pili) are specialized structures that mediate transfer of genetic material between bacteria.'),
('98d599dc-3354-5865-8338-a97464111267'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The bacterial flagellum is composed of:', '["Flagellin","Actin","Myosin","Tubulin","Keratin"]'::jsonb, 'Flagellin', 'Flagellin is the protein that makes up the bacterial flagellum, allowing bacterial motility.'),
('331317f4-8689-50df-96c7-2d9a133fd029'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which bacterium is acid-fast positive?', '["Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Streptococcus pyogenes","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Staphylococcus aureus","E. coli"]'::jsonb, 'Mycobacterium tuberculosis', 'Mycobacteria are acid-fast positive due to mycolic acids in their cell wall, which resist decolorization with acid-alcohol.'),
('20868833-62c2-55af-99b6-efbf90e5615b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Endospores are formed by which genus?', '["Salmonella","Bacillus","Neisseria","Haemophilus","Escherichia"]'::jsonb, 'Bacillus', 'Bacillus and Clostridium species form endospores, which are highly resistant dormant structures.'),
('3e148ca8-f57d-59cc-a3c1-a3e7fb5caac0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Viruses are obligate intracellular parasites because:', '["They have no cell wall clinical presentation","They cannot replicate outside a host cell","They have DNA only clinical presentation","They are too small clinical presentation","They lack enzymes clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'They cannot replicate outside a host cell', 'Viruses lack metabolic machinery and must use host cell machinery to replicate.'),
('96c4af0b-1f4d-5fd8-981a-b7b912270677'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The viral capsid is composed of:', '["Lipids","Carbohydrates","Proteins","Nucleic acids","Glycoproteins"]'::jsonb, 'Proteins', 'The capsid is the protein shell that encloses the viral genome.'),
('70915c33-a04a-55b2-be4a-46f3bbbfa91d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which virus has a helical capsid?', '["Influenza virus","Poliovirus","Herpes virus","HIV","Adenovirus"]'::jsonb, 'Influenza virus', 'Influenza virus has a helical nucleocapsid. Adenovirus and herpes have icosahedral capsids.'),
('2b241fea-833b-50d3-a32d-2e46a9c38b36'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Enveloped viruses acquire their envelope from:', '["Host cell membrane","Viral capsid","Ribosomes","Their own genome","Nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Host cell membrane', 'Enveloped viruses bud from host cell membranes, acquiring a lipid bilayer envelope with viral glycoproteins.'),
('9026247f-545d-5a13-a89a-30ba1a8cdf33'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which of the following is a DNA virus?', '["Influenza virus","HIV","Hepatitis C virus","Herpes simplex virus","Rabies virus"]'::jsonb, 'Herpes simplex virus', 'Herpes simplex virus is a double-stranded DNA virus. The others are RNA viruses.'),
('f833fe40-986b-5bf2-81e8-54d3fb5c95a1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which of the following is an RNA virus?', '["Herpes simplex","Poliovirus","Papillomavirus","Adenovirus","Hepatitis B"]'::jsonb, 'Poliovirus', 'Poliovirus is a single-stranded positive-sense RNA virus (Picornaviridae family).'),
('4476a93a-959a-5d0f-8077-c88ca616e89b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Retroviruses are characterized by:', '["Reverse transcriptase","Icosahedral capsid only","RNA-dependent RNA polymerase","DNA genome","Lack of envelope"]'::jsonb, 'Reverse transcriptase', 'Retroviruses (e.g., HIV) contain reverse transcriptase that converts RNA to DNA.'),
('1ff14fc3-f44a-50d1-aa52-bd22fb6d850d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which viral enzyme is needed for influenza virus entry?', '["Protease","Reverse transcriptase","Neuraminidase","DNA polymerase","RNA helicase"]'::jsonb, 'Neuraminidase', 'Influenza neuraminidase cleaves sialic acid residues for viral release from host cells.'),
('49516d6f-49a9-5238-a1c8-36048508154f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The size of bacteria is typically measured in:', '["Micrometers","Centimeters","Picometers","Nanometers","Millimeters"]'::jsonb, 'Micrometers', 'Bacteria are typically 0.5-5 micrometers in size. Viruses are measured in nanometers.'),
('e38b9c71-2b89-56a6-8cd6-3bc033d40ab0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Fungi are classified as:', '["Prokaryotes","Archaebacteria","Eukaryotes","Viruses","Protists"]'::jsonb, 'Eukaryotes', 'Fungi are eukaryotic organisms with chitin in their cell walls.'),
('aca9fe01-4fa1-5b1f-8b1b-c59ea50cd874'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The cell wall of fungi contains:', '["LPS","Teichoic acid","Chitin","Cellulose","Peptidoglycan"]'::jsonb, 'Chitin', 'Fungal cell walls contain chitin (a polymer of N-acetylglucosamine), cellulose, or both.'),
('636c7a93-027d-509d-90d9-13a0add656af'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which of the following is a dimorphic fungus?', '["Penicillium marneffei","Aspergillus fumigatus","Histoplasma capsulatum","Candida albicans","Mucor species"]'::jsonb, 'Histoplasma capsulatum', 'Histoplasma capsulatum is dimorphic: mold at 25°C and yeast at 37°C.'),
('2faa3ded-e0de-5186-9e79-c5af0b64d9ac'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which fungus causes opportunistic infections in immunocompromised patients?', '["Blastomyces","Coccidioides","Histoplasma","Aspergillus fumigatus","Sporothrix"]'::jsonb, 'Aspergillus fumigatus', 'Aspergillus fumigatus is an opportunistic mold causing invasive aspergillosis in immunocompromised patients.'),
('57c377de-e8cb-5f7f-bb08-2e4bbfde34e7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The trophozoite is the active feeding stage of which organism?', '["Bacteria","Virus","Prions","Fungi","Protozoa"]'::jsonb, 'Protozoa', 'Protozoa have a trophozoite (active feeding) stage and often a cyst (dormant) stage.'),
('3ab25a44-9c19-57e9-a51f-8bbc3c1712f7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which of the following is a helminth?', '["Trichomonas vaginalis","Ascaris lumbricoides","Giardia lamblia","Entamoeba histolytica","Plasmodium vivax"]'::jsonb, 'Ascaris lumbricoides', 'Ascaris lumbricoides is a roundworm (nematode). The others are protozoa.'),
('69b3dae1-38b4-586f-99fd-229628e3e03d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The definitive host for malaria parasite is:', '["Human","Mosquito","Dog","Cat","Rat"]'::jsonb, 'Mosquito', 'The female Anopheles mosquito is the definitive host where sexual reproduction of Plasmodium occurs.'),
('5c86ba68-ab5f-5d87-9606-f6e62678b318'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which parasite causes visceral leishmaniasis (Kala-azar)?', '["Leishmania tropica","Leishmania infantum","Leishmania brasiliensis","Leishmania mexicana","Leishmania donovani"]'::jsonb, 'Leishmania donovani', 'Leishmania donovani causes visceral leishmaniasis (Kala-azar), transmitted by the sandfly.'),
('ec0c02a0-af4c-53a7-8068-1efcfe18aef2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The infective stage of Entamoeba histolytica is:', '["Trophozoite","Sporozoite","Merozoite","Oocyst","Cyst"]'::jsonb, 'Cyst', 'The cyst is the infective stage of E. histolytica. Trophozoites are the invasive stage.'),
('65e1908d-83f3-5d64-8e06-5919fc10ebe1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which stain is used to detect acid-fast bacilli?', '["India ink stain","PAS stain","Giemsa stain","Gram stain","Ziehl-Neelsen stain"]'::jsonb, 'Ziehl-Neelsen stain', 'Ziehl-Neelsen stain uses carbol fuchsin with heat to stain mycobacteria.'),
('15ab5ca0-e5e1-51c3-99d9-6defeb922f81'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Electron microscopy is needed to visualize:', '["Viruses","All microbes","Bacteria","Parasites","Fungi"]'::jsonb, 'Viruses', 'Viruses are too small (<300 nm) to be seen with light microscopy and require electron microscopy.'),
('c1b019ea-51da-5e12-af86-2e1a2f5c86c2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Growth on MacConkey agar with pink colonies indicates:', '["Lactose-fermenting Gram-negative rods","Yeast","Gram-positive cocci","Gram-positive rods","Non-lactose fermenters"]'::jsonb, 'Lactose-fermenting Gram-negative rods', 'Lactose fermenters (e.g., E. coli) produce pink colonies on MacConkey agar due to acid production.'),
('8613c5de-28bf-56ea-ab0d-c2df6c0fc5e6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Catalase test is positive in:', '["Streptococcus","Staphylococcus","Lactobacillus","Bacteroides","Clostridium"]'::jsonb, 'Staphylococcus', 'Staphylococci are catalase-positive. Streptococci are catalase-negative.'),
('66798807-0d72-5492-918a-f387e84876ab'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Coagulase test is used to identify:', '["Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","E. coli","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Streptococcus pyogenes"]'::jsonb, 'Staphylococcus aureus', 'S. aureus produces coagulase, which converts fibrinogen to fibrin, distinguishing it from other staphylococci.'),
('cada7618-3982-57e3-a11d-9209c488ba35'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The oxidase test is positive in:', '["Klebsiella pneumoniae","Proteus mirabilis","Salmonella typhi","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","E. coli"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa', 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Neisseria species are oxidase-positive.'),
('b76f8561-a019-5121-8d69-ef614dbe61a4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Alpha-hemolysis on blood agar is characterized by:', '["Complete hemolysis clinical presentation","Double hemolysis clinical presentation","Partial hemolysis with green discoloration","No hemolysis clinical presentation","Beta hemolysis clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Partial hemolysis with green discoloration', 'Alpha-hemolysis shows partial hemolysis with greenish discoloration around colonies (e.g., S. pneumoniae).'),
('c127ec46-d6cb-52ce-bd6c-92c167fe55a7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which bacteria require X and V factors for growth?', '["E. coli","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Haemophilus influenzae"]'::jsonb, 'Haemophilus influenzae', 'H. influenzae requires X factor (hemin) and V factor (NAD) for growth, hence grows on chocolate agar.'),
('68a8a579-3fd7-5524-9596-1b85b51a14b4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Obligate anaerobes cannot grow in the presence of:', '["Methane","Nitrogen","Carbon dioxide","Hydrogen","Oxygen"]'::jsonb, 'Oxygen', 'Obligate anaerobes lack enzymes to detoxify oxygen free radicals and cannot survive in oxygen.'),
('70d2650f-038c-5f6e-addf-5683d809dee6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which of the following is an obligate intracellular bacterium?', '["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Chlamydia trachomatis","E. coli","Staphylococcus aureus","Bacillus anthracis"]'::jsonb, 'Chlamydia trachomatis', 'Chlamydia trachomatis is an obligate intracellular bacterium that cannot produce ATP.'),
('0eb70350-3b5f-5ba4-bebd-5d6c8e1963ee'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Rickettsia species are transmitted by:', '["Arthropod vectors","Blood transfusion","Respiratory droplets","Sexual contact","Fecal-oral route"]'::jsonb, 'Arthropod vectors', 'Rickettsia are transmitted by arthropod vectors (lice, fleas, ticks, mites).'),
('66445156-f885-5e4a-86b7-1f403a30f216'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which bacterium produces a toxin that blocks acetylcholine release?', '["E. coli","Clostridium botulinum","Staphylococcus aureus","Clostridium tetani","Bacillus cereus"]'::jsonb, 'Clostridium botulinum', 'Botulinum toxin blocks acetylcholine release at neuromuscular junctions, causing flaccid paralysis.'),
('0fdec683-d025-59f0-9226-2b5c52128432'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Tetanus toxin acts by:', '["Blocking Na+ channels","Increasing cAMP","Inhibiting glycine release","Activating adenylate cyclase","Blocking ACh release"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting glycine release', 'Tetanospasmin blocks release of inhibitory neurotransmitters (glycine, GABA), causing spastic paralysis.'),
('f2ff5e06-d7f3-54f7-9269-45d8dc67b05d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which bacteria produce enterotoxins causing traveler''s diarrhea?', '["Enterotoxigenic E. coli","Campylobacter jejuni","Salmonella typhi","Vibrio cholerae","Shigella dysenteriae"]'::jsonb, 'Enterotoxigenic E. coli', 'ETEC produces heat-labile (LT) and heat-stable (ST) enterotoxins causing watery diarrhea.'),
('3df8b165-677e-5fdd-b430-3d6a872e5f9d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Cholera toxin activates which second messenger?', '["IP3","Calcium","cAMP","DAG","cGMP"]'::jsonb, 'cAMP', 'Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates Gs protein, constitutively activating adenylate cyclase and increasing cAMP.'),
('3d18f44a-654d-5dc2-a351-c2fa36b4ddf4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Vibrio cholerae is characterized as:', '["Gram-positive rod","Gram-negative curved rod","Gram-positive coccus","Spiral bacterium","Gram-negative coccus"]'::jsonb, 'Gram-negative curved rod', 'V. cholerae is a Gram-negative, comma-shaped curved rod.'),
('750c591b-a1d0-571a-87cb-07e575bd38c6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Mycobacterium tuberculosis is characterized by:', '["Acid-fast staining","Gram-positive","Motility","Fast growth","Lack of cell wall"]'::jsonb, 'Acid-fast staining', 'M. tuberculosis is an acid-fast bacillus with slow growth (2-8 weeks on Lowenstein-Jensen medium).'),
('30b8ec30-4289-58d1-996c-e8ec09902eff'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The lipid-rich cell wall of mycobacteria contains:', '["Peptidoglycan","Mycolic acid","LPS","Teichoic acid","Chitin"]'::jsonb, 'Mycolic acid', 'Mycolic acids are long-chain fatty acids in the mycobacterial cell wall responsible for acid-fastness.'),
('d5e3bf11-8840-5654-9f73-ca437262a3e6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Leprosy is caused by:', '["Mycobacterium avium","Mycobacterium kansasii","Mycobacterium bovis","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Mycobacterium leprae"]'::jsonb, 'Mycobacterium leprae', 'M. leprae causes leprosy (Hansen disease), affecting skin and peripheral nerves.'),
('97464e7a-0c99-5dda-aaad-b27d783c4ae8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Treponema pallidum is the causative agent of:', '["Chlamydia","Chancroid","Syphilis","Lymphogranuloma venereum","Gonorrhea"]'::jsonb, 'Syphilis', 'T. pallidum is a spirochete causing syphilis. It cannot be cultured in vitro.'),
('41af3a57-7fd3-5874-90fe-045b878e6151'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The primary stage of syphilis presents with:', '["Generalized lymphadenopathy","Neurosyphilis","Gumma","Maculopapular rash","Chancre"]'::jsonb, 'Chancre', 'Primary syphilis presents with a painless chancre at the site of inoculation.'),
('2dbccc9d-1a38-526c-9057-db9cc49a8992'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which bacteria typically causes urinary tract infections?', '["Clostridium difficile","Bacteroides fragilis","Escherichia coli","Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pneumoniae"]'::jsonb, 'Escherichia coli', 'E. coli is the most common cause of community-acquired UTIs (80-85% of cases).'),
('721740c2-4869-5cad-9c50-c678dff0f6fc'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Neisseria meningitidis is a common cause of:', '["Osteomyelitis","Meningitis","Wound infection","Pneumonia","UTI"]'::jsonb, 'Meningitis', 'N. meningitidis is a leading cause of bacterial meningitis in children and young adults.'),
('045e471f-e7f3-5fc8-a21f-da6754a06949'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which bacteria causes gas gangrene?', '["Clostridium septicum","Clostridium perfringens","Clostridium botulinum","Clostridium difficile","Clostridium tetani"]'::jsonb, 'Clostridium perfringens', 'C. perfringens produces alpha-toxin (lecithinase) causing gas gangrene with crepitus.'),
('9f4e1dad-4475-5660-8b48-d8ce327c5656'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which bacteria is associated with peptic ulcer disease?', '["Helicobacter pylori","E. coli","Campylobacter jejuni","Yersinia enterocolitica","Salmonella typhi"]'::jsonb, 'Helicobacter pylori', 'H. pylori is a Gram-negative spiral bacterium that colonizes gastric mucosa causing gastritis and ulcers.'),
('0a8bee74-7737-5396-812f-4959597ea087'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa is intrinsically resistant to:', '["Penicillin","Carbapenems","Gentamicin","Ciprofloxacin","Colistin"]'::jsonb, 'Penicillin', 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa is intrinsically resistant to penicillin due to beta-lactamase production.'),
('a1718bb0-e842-52e8-a922-83151d84ded9'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which bacteria is the most common cause of hospital-acquired pneumonia?', '["Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus","Acinetobacter baumannii"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa', 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a common cause of nosocomial pneumonia in ventilated patients.'),
('d8e3f516-1201-5e3a-b45e-b3c649b3d2e4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The dark-field microscope is used to visualize:', '["Treponema pallidum","Fungi","Acid-fast bacilli","Viruses","Chlamydia"]'::jsonb, 'Treponema pallidum', 'Dark-field microscopy is used to visualize live spirochetes like T. pallidum.'),
('6884ff91-646a-51ff-bd61-3c8d6d6c2169'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which bacteria forms a ''safety pin'' appearance on staining?', '["E. coli","Yersinia pestis","Bacillus anthracis","Clostridium tetani","Neisseria meningitidis"]'::jsonb, 'Yersinia pestis', 'Y. pestis shows bipolar staining (safety pin appearance) on Wright-Giemsa stain.'),
('32896171-78c0-597f-a9df-ed01ae857a6e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Streptococcus pneumoniae is distinguished by:', '["Bile solubility and optochin sensitivity","Coagulase production clinical presentation","Spore formation clinical presentation","Catalase production clinical presentation","Motility clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Bile solubility and optochin sensitivity', 'S. pneumoniae is bile-soluble and optochin-sensitive, differentiating it from other viridans streptococci.'),
('cfca5dfd-2049-55a1-a206-ea98ae8b924f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common surgical site infection pathogen is:', '["E. coli","Bacteroides fragilis","Staphylococcus aureus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Streptococcus pyogenes"]'::jsonb, 'Staphylococcus aureus', 'S. aureus is the most common cause of surgical site infections worldwide.'),
('db462960-19e1-5a03-beba-d605129de95c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism is commonly associated with intra-abdominal abscesses?', '["S. aureus","Bacteroides fragilis","E. coli","H. influenzae","S. pneumoniae"]'::jsonb, 'Bacteroides fragilis', 'B. fragilis is the most common anaerobe in intra-abdominal infections and abscesses.'),
('79b12e73-b07a-5f73-8603-c8694d145beb'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Necrotizing fasciitis is most commonly caused by:', '["S. aureus","S. pyogenes","E. coli","P. aeruginosa","C. perfringens"]'::jsonb, 'S. pyogenes', 'Group A Streptococcus (S. pyogenes) is the most common cause of necrotizing fasciitis.'),
('c3ac1714-b890-5a26-b09d-c6e8b0cc9aef'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Gas in tissues is characteristic of infection with:', '["Bacteroides fragilis","E. coli","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","S. aureus","Clostridium perfringens"]'::jsonb, 'Clostridium perfringens', 'C. perfringens produces gas (CO2 and hydrogen) causing crepitus in gas gangrene.'),
('c1e9ae9b-fb41-5f89-8696-8adad29e5838'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism commonly infects prosthetic joints?', '["Staphylococcus epidermidis","H. influenzae","N. meningitidis","S. pneumoniae","E. coli"]'::jsonb, 'Staphylococcus epidermidis', 'S. epidermidis, a coagulase-negative staphylococcus, is a common cause of prosthetic joint infections.'),
('3190c265-7188-549e-a653-81058819a85e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common cause of catheter-associated UTI is:', '["Proteus mirabilis","S. aureus","Enterococcus faecalis","E. coli","Klebsiella pneumoniae"]'::jsonb, 'E. coli', 'E. coli is the most common cause of catheter-associated UTIs.'),
('4e193814-ee02-50c2-bbfd-ba4aae36cbf8'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism is commonly cultured from bile in cholecystitis?', '["E. coli","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","S. aureus","Clostridium perfringens","Bacteroides fragilis"]'::jsonb, 'E. coli', 'E. coli is the most common organism isolated from bile in acute cholecystitis.'),
('572463ce-b56d-53e7-8314-902c49fd83f0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common pathogen causing peritonitis in CAPD patients is:', '["S. epidermidis","E. coli","S. aureus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Candida albicans"]'::jsonb, 'S. epidermidis', 'Coagulase-negative staphylococci like S. epidermidis are the most common cause of CAPD peritonitis.'),
('4b4fc758-7a78-5750-ae29-82c0327bb88f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which anaerobe is most commonly found in brain abscesses?', '["Bacteroides fragilis","Clostridium perfringens","Peptostreptococcus","Fusobacterium nucleatum","Prevotella melaninogenica"]'::jsonb, 'Fusobacterium nucleatum', 'Fusobacterium nucleatum is commonly isolated from brain abscesses of dental origin.'),
('7cc85ee9-9e37-5172-8ca4-c979cae90c67'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Actinomyces israelii causes:', '["Actinomycosis with sulfur granules","Syphilis clinical presentation","Gas gangrene clinical presentation","Leprosy clinical presentation","Tuberculosis clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Actinomycosis with sulfur granules', 'Actinomycosis produces sulfur granules and sinuses, commonly in the cervicofacial region.'),
('a5f195a9-a0ac-5aca-9f34-ee42be2e3634'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism is a common cause of post-splenectomy sepsis?', '["S. aureus","S. pneumoniae","E. coli","H. influenzae","N. meningitidis"]'::jsonb, 'S. pneumoniae', 'S. pneumoniae is the most common cause of overwhelming post-splenectomy infection (OPSI).'),
('2bd56461-276e-5bb0-bce0-9e73dfd0accd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common pathogen in acute appendicitis is:', '["S. aureus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Bacteroides fragilis","Enterococcus faecalis","E. coli"]'::jsonb, 'E. coli', 'E. coli is the most common aerobic organism in acute appendicitis, often mixed with anaerobes.'),
('c543fc4d-e1c0-53a8-a3e3-76a4682ab46c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism is associated with ''psoas abscess''?', '["M. tuberculosis","Bacteroides fragilis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","S. aureus","E. coli"]'::jsonb, 'M. tuberculosis', 'M. tuberculosis is a common cause of psoas abscess (cold abscess) in developing countries.'),
('10881048-943b-53bc-8de4-05da503c8bf3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Bite wound infections are commonly caused by:', '["E. coli","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Pasteurella multocida","S. aureus alone","Clostridium tetani"]'::jsonb, 'Pasteurella multocida', 'Pasteurella multocida is the most common isolate from dog and cat bite wound infections.'),
('9f4b4e39-03e7-5911-a8fa-442cc445ec15'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common organism in diabetic foot infections is:', '["E. coli","S. aureus","Polymicrobial","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Bacteroides fragilis"]'::jsonb, 'Polymicrobial', 'Diabetic foot infections are typically polymicrobial with both aerobes and anaerobes.'),
('7378f683-aae4-5325-833e-da7f6e0e30e3'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism causes ''malignant otitis externa''?', '["Aspergillus niger","Proteus mirabilis","S. aureus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","E. coli"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa', 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa causes malignant otitis externa in diabetic patients.'),
('4453bd96-02ca-5739-b4ba-1abc0d94f4bf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Empyema of the gallbladder is most commonly caused by:', '["S. aureus","E. coli","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Enterococcus faecalis"]'::jsonb, 'E. coli', 'E. coli is the most common organism in empyema of the gallbladder.'),
('bd83179a-e23c-5c20-aea5-d6a29a1e5ee0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism is responsible for post-traumatic wound infections?', '["None","All of the above","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Clostridium tetani","S. aureus"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Post-traumatic wounds can be infected by S. aureus, Clostridium tetani, Pseudomonas, and many others.'),
('f1d82bbb-56dd-5bb5-91ef-e6917290d43c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common cause of osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease is:', '["S. aureus","E. coli","Salmonella species","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","S. pneumoniae"]'::jsonb, 'Salmonella species', 'Salmonella osteomyelitis is classic in sickle cell disease due to bone infarcts.'),
('7100bcc8-0965-530c-9875-e34fd1378bef'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism causes ''Pseudomembranous colitis''?', '["S. aureus","E. coli O157:H7","Clostridium difficile","Shigella flexneri","Candida albicans"]'::jsonb, 'Clostridium difficile', 'C. difficile produces toxins A and B causing pseudomembranous colitis after antibiotic use.'),
('97e4a844-788a-55cb-b237-11ce827a2e2d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common pathogen in liver abscesses is:', '["Entamoeba histolytica","Klebsiella pneumoniae","S. aureus","Bacteroides fragilis","E. coli"]'::jsonb, 'Entamoeba histolytica', 'Entamoeba histolytica causes amebic liver abscess, common in tropical regions.'),
('d8ba6d03-b4bd-573d-8b9e-c4008e5dd2da'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism causes ''waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome''?', '["E. coli","H. influenzae","N. meningitidis","S. aureus","S. pneumoniae"]'::jsonb, 'N. meningitidis', 'N. meningitidis causes Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome with bilateral adrenal hemorrhage.'),
('2f053b5b-7283-5cc6-bd40-3fcb908e1bec'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common cause of infectious endocarditis in IV drug users is:', '["Enterococcus faecalis","S. viridans","S. aureus","S. epidermidis","Candida albicans"]'::jsonb, 'S. aureus', 'S. aureus is the most common cause of endocarditis in IV drug users, typically affecting the tricuspid valve.'),
('4050d59b-31a7-527d-b131-d3c350c00afe'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Subacute bacterial endocarditis is most commonly caused by:', '["S. aureus","H. influenzae","S. viridans","Enterococcus faecalis","S. epidermidis"]'::jsonb, 'S. viridans', 'S. viridans (oral streptococci) is the most common cause of subacute bacterial endocarditis.'),
('48929145-712d-5031-a242-1e5c21e67051'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism causes ''erythrasma''?', '["E. coli","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","S. aureus","Corynebacterium minutissimum","S. pyogenes"]'::jsonb, 'Corynebacterium minutissimum', 'Corynebacterium minutissimum causes erythrasma, a superficial skin infection in intertriginous areas.'),
('91d5f900-4ff6-5d82-b84a-6f8d67484939'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which bacteria is associated with ''Fournier gangrene''?', '["Polymicrobial","Clostridium perfringens","Bacteroides fragilis","S. aureus","E. coli"]'::jsonb, 'Polymicrobial', 'Fournier gangrene is a polymicrobial necrotizing fasciitis of the perineum.'),
('0f994bd7-da53-51fd-ac34-8ae49a83ff2a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common organism in infected pancreatic necrosis is:', '["Pseudomonas aeruginosa","S. aureus","E. coli","Candida albicans","Enterococcus faecalis"]'::jsonb, 'E. coli', 'E. coli and other Gram-negative enteric bacteria commonly infect pancreatic necrosis.'),
('5374702c-90c2-5941-a945-ef1144c08fed'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism causes ''ludwig angina''?', '["Bacteroides fragilis","Clostridium perfringens","S. pyogenes","Polymicrobial","S. aureus"]'::jsonb, 'Polymicrobial', 'Ludwig angina is a polymicrobial infection of the submandibular space from dental sources.'),
('78f96ae1-e782-5cde-8c1b-05d44ca2d2a0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common cause of infective endocarditis on prosthetic valves within 1 year is:', '["S. aureus","S. epidermidis","S. viridans","Enterococcus faecalis","Candida albicans"]'::jsonb, 'S. epidermidis', 'S. epidermidis is the most common cause of early prosthetic valve endocarditis (<1 year).'),
('fcf0077a-1327-59cf-b65f-260dfee33abe'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism causes ''tetanus''?', '["Clostridium perfringens","Clostridium tetani","Clostridium botulinum","Clostridium difficile","Clostridium septicum"]'::jsonb, 'Clostridium tetani', 'C. tetani produces tetanospasmin, a neurotoxin causing generalized spastic paralysis.'),
('fc373c97-e293-5b03-8845-dd7a6f107b0d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Nocardia asteroides is characterized by:', '["Gram-positive branching filaments","Yeast-like growth clinical presentation","Acid-fast bacilli clinical presentation","Gram-negative rods clinical presentation","Spore formation clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Gram-positive branching filaments', 'Nocardia are Gram-positive, branching filamentous bacteria that are partially acid-fast.'),
('144376dd-fd5d-570f-a23a-8a992a75769b'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism is most commonly isolated from chronic osteomyelitis?', '["Bacteroides fragilis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","S. aureus","S. epidermidis","E. coli"]'::jsonb, 'S. aureus', 'S. aureus is the most common isolate in both acute and chronic osteomyelitis.'),
('72c23d31-efa9-556a-8b46-3f1dfd94ea36'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism causes ''bacterial vaginosis''?', '["Chlamydia trachomatis","Candida albicans","Gardnerella vaginalis","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Treponema pallidum"]'::jsonb, 'Gardnerella vaginalis', 'Gardnerella vaginalis is associated with bacterial vaginosis with characteristic clue cells.'),
('2b6ad6aa-3c67-504a-820e-d583b5a7edf6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common cause of urinary tract infection in catheterized patients is:', '["Enterococcus faecalis","Klebsiella pneumoniae","E. coli","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Proteus mirabilis"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa', 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a common cause of catheter-associated UTI in hospitalized patients.'),
('3b5f82a1-1de6-5092-95c1-ee78de0e3e66'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism causes ''surgical emphysema'' in gas gangrene?', '["C. tetani","C. perfringens","C. botulinum","C. difficile","C. septicum"]'::jsonb, 'C. perfringens', 'C. perfringens produces gas (hydrogen and CO2) causing surgical emphysema with crepitus.'),
('84f9442c-93ae-5ac0-a82c-21a59a7227a6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common pathogen in perianal abscesses is:', '["E. coli","Bacteroides fragilis","S. aureus","S. epidermidis","Polymicrobial"]'::jsonb, 'Polymicrobial', 'Perianal abscesses are typically polymicrobial with mixed aerobic and anaerobic flora.'),
('d0d67511-4341-5f5e-ac6a-8a960affcfac'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism is associated with ''hospital-acquired pneumonia''?', '["H. influenzae","M. catarrhalis","C. pneumoniae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","S. pneumoniae"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa', 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a leading cause of hospital-acquired pneumonia, especially in ICU patients.'),
('9c77ff83-823b-5024-8235-849c65cddd0a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism causes ''melioidosis''?', '["Acinetobacter baumannii","Stenotrophomonas maltophilia","Burkholderia pseudomallei","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Burkholderia cepacia"]'::jsonb, 'Burkholderia pseudomallei', 'Burkholderia pseudomallei causes melioidosis, endemic in Southeast Asia and northern Australia.'),
('41fd8970-a457-5d82-8ded-6ff2c1a8eeea'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common cause of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis is:', '["E. coli","S. pneumoniae","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Enterococcus faecalis","Bacteroides fragilis"]'::jsonb, 'E. coli', 'E. coli is the most common cause of SBP in cirrhotic patients with ascites.'),
('ffbe2d32-0a8e-5c71-bd64-5e43c84d3b70'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which organism causes ''erysipelas''?', '["S. aureus","S. pyogenes","S. pneumoniae","E. coli","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"]'::jsonb, 'S. pyogenes', 'Erysipelas is a superficial cellulitis caused by S. pyogenes, often on the face or legs.'),
('20b9c3f9-7d64-5751-93b3-3517c21ebc37'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Hepatitis B virus is a:', '["RNA virus","Prion","Delta virus","Retrovirus","DNA virus"]'::jsonb, 'DNA virus', 'HBV is a partially double-stranded DNA virus of the Hepadnaviridae family.'),
('1c0c3dfe-4609-5a8a-bb5f-4531d82da527'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which antigen of HBV indicates active viral replication?', '["HBcAg","HBxAg","HBeAg","HBsAg","Pre-S1 antigen"]'::jsonb, 'HBeAg', 'HBeAg positivity correlates with active viral replication and high infectivity.'),
('817db8b8-2141-5af1-ae90-9aaac0b8799a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common mode of HBV transmission in Pakistan is:', '["Vector-borne","Sexual only","Fecal-oral","Respiratory","Parenteral and vertical"]'::jsonb, 'Parenteral and vertical', 'In Pakistan, HBV is commonly transmitted via contaminated needles (parenteral) and from mother to child (vertical).'),
('ab615628-b041-56f7-92ae-be188ab4a82c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which HBV serologic marker appears first after infection?', '["HBsAg","Anti-HBs","Anti-HBc IgM","HBeAg","Anti-HBe"]'::jsonb, 'HBsAg', 'HBsAg is the first marker to appear, detectable 2-8 weeks before symptom onset.'),
('9e35a6c6-6edf-5ff2-af70-96cb60f16951'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The presence of anti-HBs indicates:', '["Reactivation","Chronic carrier state","Active infection","Acute infection","Past infection or vaccination"]'::jsonb, 'Past infection or vaccination', 'Anti-HBs is the protective antibody and indicates immunity from past infection or vaccination.'),
('2f90d342-d3d6-5af2-bb56-5cd1305f6859'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which serologic pattern indicates chronic HBV infection?', '["Anti-HBc IgG only","HBsAg positive with anti-HBs","All negative","Anti-HBs positive","HBsAg positive for >6 months"]'::jsonb, 'HBsAg positive for >6 months', 'HBsAg persisting for more than 6 months defines chronic HBV infection.'),
('f2811dcf-9f86-5994-8a00-e22dfeae9986'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Hepatitis B vaccination is recommended for:', '["All healthcare workers","Children only","Elderly only","Pregnant women only","No one"]'::jsonb, 'All healthcare workers', 'HBV vaccination is recommended for all healthcare workers and high-risk groups.'),
('e5530965-4de4-5c1c-b54c-420cbad7bb1c'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which HBV marker is present in the serum during the ''window period''?', '["HBsAg","Anti-HBs","Anti-HBc IgM","HBeAg","Anti-HBe"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-HBc IgM', 'During the window period, HBsAg is negative and anti-HBs is not yet positive, but anti-HBc IgM is detectable.'),
('8860ed14-1e7b-5267-9f17-b79c23c112ce'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Hepatitis B e antigen (HBeAg) is a marker of:', '["Chronic carrier state only","Past infection","Vaccination","Viral replication and infectivity","Immunity"]'::jsonb, 'Viral replication and infectivity', 'HBeAg is a marker of active viral replication and high infectivity.'),
('8c893cfe-5f83-5848-93e8-1e12c15b0f27'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The risk of chronic HBV infection is highest in:', '["Immunocompetent adults","Infants and children under 5","Elderly","Adolescents","Adults"]'::jsonb, 'Infants and children under 5', '90% of infants infected at birth become chronic carriers vs. <5% of immunocompetent adults.'),
('c33287d7-1d77-5b19-bbd7-e26d1b5e466a'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Extrahepatic manifestations of HBV include:', '["Polyarteritis nodosa","Rheumatoid arthritis","Systemic lupus","Sarcoidosis","Ankylosing spondylitis"]'::jsonb, 'Polyarteritis nodosa', 'HBV can cause polyarteritis nodosa and membranous glomerulonephritis as extrahepatic manifestations.'),
('ec796e77-e4ab-5732-bd29-2cd4d4486073'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Fulminant hepatitis B is associated with:', '["Co-infection with HDV","HBV vaccination","Anti-HBs positivity","HBV alone","Past HBV infection"]'::jsonb, 'Co-infection with HDV', 'Co-infection with hepatitis D virus (HDV) increases the risk of fulminant hepatitis B.'),
('71a279d8-c2cc-53b3-bc32-cf4e216b0151'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Hepatitis C virus is a:', '["DNA virus","Single-stranded RNA virus","Double-stranded RNA virus","Retrovirus","Parvovirus"]'::jsonb, 'Single-stranded RNA virus', 'HCV is a single-stranded positive-sense RNA virus of the Flaviviridae family.'),
('ddacf106-f55e-51ad-aa83-a95d408c69ec'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common genotype of HCV in Pakistan is:', '["Genotype 4","Genotype 5","Genotype 2","Genotype 1","Genotype 3"]'::jsonb, 'Genotype 3', 'HCV genotype 3 is the most common genotype in Pakistan, accounting for 80-85% of cases.'),
('aa1767b7-9cf3-5333-83a1-9ba1d6b9004e'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Chronic HCV infection is defined as:', '["HCV RNA positive for >6 months","HCC development","Anti-HCV positive","Cirrhosis","Abnormal LFTs"]'::jsonb, 'HCV RNA positive for >6 months', 'Persistent HCV RNA in blood for more than 6 months defines chronic HCV infection.'),
('ae8a6bfe-41ea-5e8e-a4c0-e8a6c0d3a1cd'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common mode of HCV transmission in Pakistan is:', '["Nosocomial clinical presentation","Fecal-oral clinical presentation","Vertical clinical presentation","Sexual clinical presentation","Blood transfusion and unsafe injections"]'::jsonb, 'Blood transfusion and unsafe injections', 'Unsafe therapeutic injections and blood transfusions are the main modes of HCV transmission in Pakistan.'),
('76995b5b-db2d-5227-ba26-a620df3beed4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'What is the rate of chronicity after acute HCV infection?', '["About 15%","About 50%","About 75-85%","Nearly 100%","About 5%"]'::jsonb, 'About 75-85%', 'Approximately 75-85% of acute HCV infections progress to chronic infection.'),
('c66eb4b3-55ca-55c5-927d-5f1cc2184100'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which of the following is a direct-acting antiviral for HCV?', '["Sofosbuvir","Ribavirin","Interferon-alpha","Tenofovir","Lamivudine"]'::jsonb, 'Sofosbuvir', 'Sofosbuvir is a direct-acting antiviral (NS5B polymerase inhibitor) used in HCV treatment.'),
('543fa0f4-f8f7-5b52-b0fb-3e8b4b398b39'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'HCV is associated with which extrahepatic manifestation?', '["Polymyositis","Rheumatic fever","Sjogren syndrome","Systemic sclerosis","Mixed cryoglobulinemia"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed cryoglobulinemia', 'Mixed cryoglobulinemia type II is a common extrahepatic manifestation of chronic HCV infection.'),
('a9853d5d-286c-5f7a-8c37-54bb379a26c1'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which HCV protein is used for diagnostic antibody testing?', '["Core","E1/E2","NS3","NS5","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Anti-HCV antibody tests use recombinant proteins from core, NS3, NS4, and NS5 regions.'),
('e501617c-eaa7-520d-b9dc-f657d8ae12ee'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The annual risk of HCC in HCV-related cirrhosis is:', '["50%","1-4%","10-15%","<1%","20-30%"]'::jsonb, '1-4%', 'The annual incidence of HCC in HCV cirrhotic patients is 1-4% per year.'),
('51780632-701a-5e12-a58c-2659f59e9d79'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which HBV vaccine is most commonly used?', '["Toxoid","Recombinant HBsAg","Inactivated virus","Live attenuated virus","DNA vaccine"]'::jsonb, 'Recombinant HBsAg', 'The HBV vaccine is a recombinant HBsAg produced in yeast cells.'),
('939238a7-e80e-5ca7-ac58-2ea9756fb668'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which of the following is true about HBV DNA?', '["It indicates viral load","It is not used for monitoring","It only appears in cirrhosis","It is always detectable in carriers","It disappears after 1 year"]'::jsonb, 'It indicates viral load', 'HBV DNA quantification is used to assess viral replication and guide treatment decisions.'),
('f2a26833-a0b1-575d-8628-db3094141c75'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Hepatitis D virus requires which helper virus?', '["HBV","HEV","HCV","HGV","HAV"]'::jsonb, 'HBV', 'HDV is a defective RNA virus that requires HBV (HBsAg) for its replication.'),
('5d8af378-d46f-5fba-b39d-47632502076d'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'The most common cause of acute viral hepatitis in Pakistan is:', '["HEV","HAV","HBV","HDV","HCV"]'::jsonb, 'HAV', 'HAV (fecal-oral transmission) is the most common cause of acute viral hepatitis in children in Pakistan.'),
('08f0aadb-9407-5a4f-a6e0-5883436183e4'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which hepatitis virus causes epidemic outbreaks through contaminated water?', '["HDV","HAV and HEV","HGV","HBV and HCV","TTV"]'::jsonb, 'HAV and HEV', 'HAV and HEV are transmitted via the fecal-oral route and cause water-borne outbreaks.'),
('febe6604-5948-5fd0-a5dc-81ff438352c2'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Hepatitis E virus is most severe in:', '["Children","Elderly","Men","Pregnant women","Immunocompromised"]'::jsonb, 'Pregnant women', 'HEV has a high mortality rate (15-25%) in pregnant women, especially in the third trimester.'),
('078c1ef1-e0cd-5c20-b864-3ee1da913954'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which HBV mutation is associated with vaccine escape?', '["Core gene mutation","S gene mutation","Polymerase mutation","X gene mutation","Pre-core mutation"]'::jsonb, 'S gene mutation', 'S gene mutation in HBsAg can lead to vaccine escape and diagnostic failure.'),
('5bd34617-0015-55e0-b712-3e066f2c91b0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'HCV leads to cirrhosis in what percentage of chronic cases?', '["20-30%","50-60%",">80%","<5%","10-20%"]'::jsonb, '10-20%', 'Approximately 10-20% of chronic HCV patients develop cirrhosis over 20-30 years.'),
('99c23a36-32bc-59ec-ae07-8e112616bad5'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'What is the most common cause of chronic hepatitis in Pakistan?', '["HBV","HCV","HAV","HEV","Alcohol"]'::jsonb, 'HCV', 'HCV is the most common cause of chronic hepatitis (genotype 3 predominant) in Pakistan.'),
('e128b181-6111-502f-9ca4-1beefe49211f'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Herpes simplex virus type 1 causes:', '["Shingles","Genital herpes","Chickenpox","Cytomegalovirus disease","Oral herpes"]'::jsonb, 'Oral herpes', 'HSV-1 typically causes orolabial herpes. HSV-2 typically causes genital herpes.'),
('e5c1f6ec-48a3-58ac-98f8-87c3b3aa25e0'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Varicella zoster virus causes:', '["Molluscum contagiosum","Genital warts","Oral herpes only","Chickenpox and shingles","Fifth disease"]'::jsonb, 'Chickenpox and shingles', 'VZV causes chickenpox (primary) and shingles (reactivation from dorsal root ganglia).'),
('69a93eba-4924-5c46-a8e6-eda48685f9ea'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which virus causes infectious mononucleosis?', '["HHV-6","Herpes simplex virus","HHV-8","Epstein-Barr virus","Cytomegalovirus"]'::jsonb, 'Epstein-Barr virus', 'EBV causes infectious mononucleosis (glandular fever) with fever, lymphadenopathy, and pharyngitis.'),
('8a7b1590-dda0-52b2-9e7c-4ce770326ad7'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Cytomegalovirus infection in immunocompromised patients most commonly causes:', '["Encephalitis","Skin rash","Lymphadenopathy","Pneumonitis and retinitis","Hepatitis"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumonitis and retinitis', 'CMV causes pneumonitis, retinitis, and colitis in immunocompromised patients (AIDS, transplant).'),
('d66eb8ae-dcd1-55f6-9029-576cd1cdc718'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Human papillomavirus causes:', '["Cervical cancer","Hand-foot-mouth disease","Genital warts","Both of the above","Oral herpes"]'::jsonb, 'Both of the above', 'HPV causes genital warts (low-risk types 6,11) and cervical cancer (high-risk types 16,18).'),
('9323f057-9e3f-5b2b-958a-1f0b12e3e972'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which virus causes ''hand-foot-mouth disease''?', '["HSV-1","Coxsackievirus A16","Varicella zoster","EBV","Parvovirus B19"]'::jsonb, 'Coxsackievirus A16', 'Coxsackievirus A16 causes hand-foot-mouth disease with vesicles on palms, soles, and oral mucosa.'),
('82c42020-54e4-520e-a30d-77f51dac84ea'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Rabies virus is transmitted by:', '["Respiratory droplets","Fecal-oral route","Animal bite","Blood transfusion","Sexual contact"]'::jsonb, 'Animal bite', 'Rabies is transmitted via saliva of infected animals, typically through bites.'),
('c11f4f96-0db9-5da8-8697-9f917542d815'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Negri bodies are characteristic of:', '["Polio","Hepatitis","Herpes","Influenza","Rabies"]'::jsonb, 'Rabies', 'Negri bodies are eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions in neurons, pathognomonic of rabies.'),
('aa31684c-8978-5659-a4e9-f5ed15c2acf6'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which virus causes dengue fever?', '["Flavivirus","Bunyavirus","Togavirus","Filovirus","Orthomyxovirus"]'::jsonb, 'Flavivirus', 'Dengue virus is a Flavivirus transmitted by Aedes mosquitoes.'),
('01d7fa0c-f85e-57d1-abea-5e05e99c8ebf'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Dengue hemorrhagic fever is mediated by:', '["Antibody-dependent enhancement","Bacterial superinfection","T-cell mediated","Direct viral damage","Toxin production"]'::jsonb, 'Antibody-dependent enhancement', 'ADE occurs when non-neutralizing antibodies from primary infection enhance secondary infection.'),
('c41d68cb-7b95-5b9f-8845-3dad36fc3b97'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Which virus causes ''SARS'' (Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome)?', '["Coronavirus","Parainfluenza","Influenza A","RSV","Adenovirus"]'::jsonb, 'Coronavirus', 'SARS is caused by SARS-CoV (coronavirus), which emerged as a zoonosis from civet cats.'),
('29d00e48-7df9-5263-bc95-d319ba9d64da'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Influenza virus belongs to which family?', '["Picornaviridae","Togaviridae","Flaviviridae","Orthomyxoviridae","Paramyxoviridae"]'::jsonb, 'Orthomyxoviridae', 'Influenza virus is an Orthomyxovirus with segmented RNA genome.'),
('75e262de-61de-5ecd-9b83-bf14167b3a62'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Antigenic drift in influenza refers to:', '["Viral latency","Change in viral tropism","Change in host range","Minor changes due to point mutations","Major antigenic change due to reassortment"]'::jsonb, 'Minor changes due to point mutations', 'Antigenic drift results from point mutations in HA and NA genes, causing seasonal epidemics.'),
('10614e17-7811-54cc-8f55-daff072acf01'::uuid, 'Pathology (Paper 1)', 'Microbiology', 'Antigenic shift in influenza refers to:', '["Major reassortment producing new subtypes","Point mutations clinical presentation","Host immunity clinical presentation","Antibody escape clinical presentation","Minor changes clinical presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Major reassortment producing new subtypes', 'Antigenic shift results from reassortment of RNA segments, causing pandemics.'),
